00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.060
There are many horror fans out there who balk&nbsp;
at the thought of yet another found footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.620
movie. For some, the format has been overused&nbsp;
by too many low-budget filmmakers who don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.520
know how to make the most of it. When they're&nbsp;
at their best, though, found footage movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.200
thrust the viewer into the heart of darkness&nbsp;
and provide them with an immersive experience.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.460
According to some fans, "Skinamarink"&nbsp;
is a prime example of found footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.420
horror at its best — right up there&nbsp;
with movies like "The Blair Witch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.380
Project," "Paranormal Activity," and&nbsp;
"Cannibal Holocaust." At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:31.260
Kyle Edward Ball's feature is a beast unto itself&nbsp;
that's comparable to nothing else. One fan posted,

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.280
"Skinamarink" is a horror movie&nbsp;
that favors mood, atmosphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.560
and nightmarish qualities over a traditional&nbsp;
narrative, and explaining the plot would spoil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.480
its ghoulish shocks and surprises. All you need&nbsp;
to know is that it revolves around two children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.840
who wake up in the middle of the night&nbsp;
to discover that their father is missing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.260
and all of the windows and doors&nbsp;
to their home have disappeared.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.800
As documented by Rotten Tomatoes, many&nbsp;
reviewers have commented on the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.400
experimental qualities. In fact,&nbsp;
most viewers will probably come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.300
of "Skinamarink" scratching their heads&nbsp;
and wondering what they just watched.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.500
At the same time, the movie taps into fears&nbsp;
that most people have been able to relate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.540
at some point in their lives — namely, being&nbsp;
a child and feeling anxious about monsters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.220
the thought of our guardians disappearing. Kyle&nbsp;
Edward Ball told Inverse that the confusion is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

